  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "What does \\(\\log_a(MN)\\) equal?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\log_a M+\\log_a N\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\log_a M\\cdot\\log_a N\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\log_a M-\\log_a N\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\log_a(M+N)\\)"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Assume \\(a>0\\), \\(a\\ne1\\), \\(M>0\\), and \\(N>0\\). The logarithm of a product equals the sum of the logarithms, so\n\\[\n\\log_a(MN)=\\log_a M+\\log_a N.\n\\]\nSubtraction applies to the logarithm of a quotient.\n\nThe positive-factor assumption is missing from the question. For example, if \\(a=2\\) and \\(M=N=-1\\), then \\(\\log_a(MN)=\\log_2 1=0\\), but all four choices contain undefined real logarithms. Thus A is correct under the standard positive-factor assumption; as written, no choice is valid for every input for which the left side exists.",
